tsukai so  whatsoever you would probably think that  fushiguro was the main protagonist  of course the setting being his old  junior high and the exploration with his  sister would also contribute to that all  of which was quite enjoyable any  opportunity to expand  on him is very welcome and i did like  that nephit was made to explore his  self-sacrificial nature and there's one  quote that really hit me during the sarc  which came from a fly-looking casual  gojo  stating that being introduced sorcerer  is an individual sport no matter how  many allies you have around you you'll  always die alone which is fascinating  because as much as this was all about  fushiguro it definitely called me right  back to the very first chapter and the  words of yuji's grandfather which was a  warning to you know  not do that he very strictly said do not  die alone so what does yuji do after  that well he chooses the one profession  where dying alone is industry standard  practice  and it also brings back echoes of the  principal's initial words that no  sorcerer dies without regret so casual  fly f gojo here provided a nice hit of  morbidity and a reminder of exactly what  we're up against here it's actually been  so long that i have kind of forgotten  that the entire plan fuji was to be  executed and that's not really  commensurate with the events that we've  been through all about getting stronger  saving people and yes making the best of  friends and there's a nice extra hit of  morbidity as well because this doesn't  just apply to youtube but to all of our  students who on their multiple parts are  apparently all heading towards the same  sort of tragic end with that said in for  shigeru's case these words were quite  enlightening and led  seeing a side of him that i didn't  really think existed sort of  in a release of restraint and from there  we were treated to all sorts of fun art  of him smiling and laughing albeit in a  very  very twisted manner but i do like the  overall message that was presented here  which was give it your all it's okay to  be selfish  because you will almost never hear that  in a shorter manga as they are usually  about promoting selflessness above all  else and also it would seem that  fushiguro has  a thing i want to call it a domain but  that's not what he called it i think it  was an area  expansion i believe culminating in a  chimera shadow garden i'm going to  assume it's either like a mini domain or  an incomplete domain which i do think  fujikura quite literally states but  these are always fascinating because  they give us a glimpse of core character  a morphing shadow garden at once ornate  beautiful  versatile and terrifying and also the  idea is that shadows tend to be very  sneaky background features so all of  this is pretty perfectly for girl and i  would love to see the complete version  and i would also go into fushkar's  sister smiki but i really don't know how  much more there is to say  right now it's nice to have another  reference to her because  i think when we first saw her way back  when it was just a weird out-of-contact  shot but i guess it's good to know that  there's something in the background  motivating fushiguro along with the  whole xenon family thing but this does  feel like a setup for a later story to  play out so  i'll talk about it then not to be  neglected during the sack yuji and  kogisaki end up forming a pretty  fearsome duo and actually having stated  that the focus of this arc was very  fushiguro-centric it's these two who  receive the grand action and even  emotional climax come to think of it in  regards to the latter there's this  really striking set of four pages  towards the end of the arc where  akhitami draws a conversation between  yuji and kugasaki where we spend one  page looking at one character from the  exact same camera angle four different  times then we do the same with the other  character  then we flip back and so on in these  pages aesthetically are quite  unremarkable they're so unremarkable  that they are indeed remarkable they're  very flat the panel sizes are all  uniform and it really does go against  the core principles of manga page layout  but the real beauty of what akatami does  with these four pages  is remove all unnecessary visual flair  so that we are forced to focus on the  subtle differences of expression within  the characters going through each of  their series of panels is an emotional  journey of two characters coming to  terms with what they've done in their  different ways and a very minor thing  that does actually help is that even  though it's a static shot of each  character the forest background actually  changes with each panel which is kind of  trippy and really does make me feel like  i'm cutting to a completely new  character with each emotional shift so  it's stupidly subtle but also  very very effective and it's something  that gives me a lot of trust in  akutami's creative decision making i've  brought this up before but i'm really  starting to believe that akatami's  greatest strength as an author is  knowing what not to draw like in this  specific arc as well there's a whole  series of panels in fushigoro's mind  which are just  words featuring the kind words of samiki  backed by pristine flat white whilst  fushigoro's pessimistic thoughts are  played against this despairful flat  black i think that most mangaka would be  hesitant to do something like this  because manga is supposed to be  primarily a visual medium especially  mangaka with akutami's level of skill  and yet the restraint he shows where it  really matters is quite enlightening  because the kind of stuff that he draws  or  even types in in text during these  moments can be achieved by  literally any artist however very few of  them could use these simple choices as  effectively back to eugene kugisaki  though in fact specifically  kuisaki this time she got some fantastic  moments to shine i love seeing her in  action even if she is slightly  terrifying kugisaki just fights with  such a sinister smile it's almost  sadistic really and hey she got to  perform a black flash alongside yuji  which was pretty damn cool  who speaking of was thrown into another  moral quandary during the sark for  reasons i haven't touched on yet our  main antagonists were not cursed spirits  or at least they weren't  fully cursed spirits in fact they were  even capable of emotion which means that  yuji needed to make a serious decision  over whether or not to actually kill his  opponent which he eventually did but he  also apologized beforehand  and the whole situation was just tragedy  as per usual it did show some very solid  growth though because this is not the  first time that yuji has been forced to  kill and i suppose that his resolve has  now been far more steadied which leads  us to esso and ketchizu one of which  gave me some pretty distinct jojo vibes  for obvious reasons and here's the thing  as much as i felt for yuji and kogisaki  needing to kill them i also felt really  bad that these two needed to die in the  first place i mean sure they're  antagonists but they have such a twisted  creation story and this whole brotherly  bond going on that it was  definitely just very very sad that story  was quite fascinating though because it  introduced us to sorcery of the past  with the specific figure of nori  toshikamo being flagged aka the most  evilest  of sorcerer dudes in history which was  fun because i thought that the most evil  sorcerer in history would have been  tsukuna so  interesting to know also though isn't  noritoshikamo like the exact same name  as the guy from kyoto who we fought  against in the last arc and if so  why would you name your child after the  most evil sorcerer to have ever existed  especially if it is a stain on your  family name  it's like having harry potter name his  child voldemort why  why why would why why in any case nori  toshikamo the uh the much older one  is responsible for a cursed womb  abomination which is a concept that i'm  very glad we're bringing back  and i think the implication is that he  created nine death paintings the only  ones we've seen thus far being one to  three whatever the case  i do like history diving into stuff like  this that happened hundreds or perhaps  even thousands of years ago makes the  world feel  a lot easier to be immersed in rather  than examining a more superficial modern  day story with no depth to be exported  but in the end two of our three  paintings fall  leaving choso i think his name is who i  imagine will be rather upset with our  first years upon eventually meeting them  you can definitely tell that he was the  one intended to be a more long-term  character though because his design was  on a completely different level to the  other brothers who more or less looked  like monsters of the week they're quite  complicated though mostly because of  this whole being partly human thing it  really muddies the waters even more in  regards to the whole  r curses human discussion because they  definitely are but they're also  definitely not so where do they fit in  in any given ideology like i have a hard  time believing that cyclops spirit dude  what's his name it's like the opposite  of gojo  jogo that's it i have a hard time  believing that he would be so accepting  of them into his  neo-human world but then again old form  humans probably wouldn't be too quick to  accept them either  or would they i mean probably not but  really even our grand good guys in this  world  invoke dark curse powers so what would  be the difference in accepting someone  into society who was partially a cursed  spirit in any case those questions don't  need to be answered right now and this  harkens with a  surprisingly delightful chapter  featuring a girl that likes yuji just  rocking up out of nowhere and that was  very cute extremely out of place i don't  know why we're exploring this  just yet but it was also a really nice  break from the flood of morbidity being  thrown our way almost like a reminder of  the sort of thing that we're fighting  for because as much as the negativity of  humans have resulted in these curses  there's also plenty of good things  happening in the world of course what i  fear most however is that this  delightful innocent girl yuko  i think and i fear that she is going to  be used for some sort of major  major tragedy in the future sort of like  jinpei's mother or even better  jinpe himself she's just too likable too  normal and  as such this series is a very very  dangerous place for  yuko to be inhabiting that's not quite  it though because we did have some very  relevant revelations here as well which  is that yuji fujiguro kugisaki maki  and the bestest of the boys panda have  all been recommended for a promotion to  grade  1 sorcerers which is very cool but feels  very quick  just flying straight to the top of the  hierarchy like that i mean not the very  top there are grades above it i imagine  like special grades semi special grade  etc but still that's pretty wild  especially since as far as i can recall  yuji is completely  unranked and was anonymity a grade one  sorcerer so yeah that's pretty insanely  panda on the other hand is completely  deserving of such an accolade in fact i  think he should be a special grade  because he's a very special boy  so really what we appear to have  accomplished with the whole death  painting shenanigan re is a rather  successful bridging arc it was a nice  break from the chaotic character  juggling events of the kyoto goodwill  event arc and it was really good to  finally get back to this basic trio  dynamic of yuji fushiguru and kugisaki  something that we really haven't had  since the very beginning of the series  and overall it was just a more basic  uncomplicated character growth story  whilst also doing some work to seed  future stories  and very well done arc and the only  t